# Password Authentication in Linux

Linux stores user account information separately from password information.

- `/etc/passwd` stores basic account details.
- `/etc/shadow` stores password hashes and password aging information.

Passwords are **never stored in plaintext**. Instead, Linux stores a cryptographic hash of the password.

During authentication:

```text
User enters password

        │

        ▼

Password is hashed

        │

        ▼

Hash compared with stored hash

        │

        ▼

Authentication succeeds or fails
```

This approach prevents the operating system from storing users' actual passwords.

